WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.660
Tim Ferriss:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.320
Dr. Volkow, it is lovely to see you. Thank&nbsp;
you for making the time to be on the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.000
Thanks for being here.
Dr. Nora Volkow: Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.500
it's a pleasure. Thanks for having me.
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely. And I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.260
say before we launch into the full conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:35.760
a sincere thanks to you and the&nbsp;
entire NIDA team because I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:43.320
found addiction to be very near and dear to me in&nbsp;
the sense that my uncle died of alcohol-induced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:50.040
cardiomyopathy. One of my aunts died of alcohol&nbsp;
and Percocet as an overdose. One of my best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.440
friends growing up died of a fentanyl overdose.
So this is a subject that I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:01.800
not surprisingly to anyone listening, is&nbsp;
incredibly important and I've just had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.320
opportunity to see it in very close quarters. So&nbsp;
I wanted to start with just thanking you for doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:15.660
the work that you do. So that's step number one.
And I thought then I would take a very hard left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:24.420
turn and ask you about cadavers. Specifically,&nbsp;
I read that when you were quite young, you asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:28.680
your father if you could bring a cadaver&nbsp;
home to dissect. His answer was yes. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:34.680
that his answer was generally yes to anything&nbsp;
science-related. Could you just flesh this story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.200
out a little bit, provide a little more context?
Dr. Nora Volkow: Absolutely. And first of all, I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:46.140
sorry for the loss of your family, relatives and&nbsp;
your friend. I mean, it is very tragic and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:52.620
why I mean, I'm so devoted to the whole subject&nbsp;
because it shouldn't be. I mean, people shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:58.500
be dying from addictions and yet they do. And&nbsp;
there are many factors that if we could change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.740
then we wouldn't be losing these lives.
So then let me jump into the question that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:10.380
you call left-side or right-sided. My father&nbsp;
is a scientist and so he was obsessed of having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:14.880
his daughters, he didn't have a son, so he&nbsp;
was obsessed of having the daughters going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.760
to science. And I was the one that, I have&nbsp;
another sister who was then in high school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.320
but I was the one in medical school, and I&nbsp;
wanted to do research. And I was absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:32.880
fascinated by the human body and anatomy.
And I just marvel about the beauty of how our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:39.720
bodies are constructed. So in the classes we have&nbsp;
to take anatomy and we had to dissect actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:46.080
basically dissection on people who have died,&nbsp;
on corpses. But I didn't have enough time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:52.080
and I wanted, I'm very obsessive. Whenever&nbsp;
I do things, I do them compulsively. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:58.020
wanted to be able to do that right.
And based on the time limits that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:03.000
have at school, it was going to be impossible.&nbsp;
We would be able to, each one of us dissect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.500
for example, one extremity. And so&nbsp;
I asked my father if I could bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:13.560
a corpse that I could do autopsy. And&nbsp;
my father had a laboratory in the house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:21.840
and he said yes. But then my mother and&nbsp;
my sisters rebelled and said, "If Nora&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:29.460
brings a corpse into the house for her anatomy&nbsp;
classes, then we are leaving the house." That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:35.940
very categorical. I lost, that's it. No more.
Tim Ferriss: So I'm going to come back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:42.240
obsessive focus and following the trail where&nbsp;
it leads. I'm going to come back to that. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:48.660
before I do, perhaps you could just describe&nbsp;
where you grew up, where you were at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:55.740
and how someone or a family with the last name&nbsp;
Volkow, which to most people would perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.740
bring up connotations of certain types&nbsp;
of origins, end up in where you ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:09.480
in terms of your formative years.
Dr. Nora Volkow: I was born in Mexico City. I went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:16.200
to medical school in Mexico City. And it was until&nbsp;
I was finishing medical school that I left Mexico&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:22.680
to go to Paris, then to the jungle. And then I&nbsp;
went to New York for my training in psychiatry.&nbsp;

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:30.900
And the reason why, actually I ultimately&nbsp;
was born in Mexico City. It's interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:39.420
it's driven by, I always say, the belligerent&nbsp;
nature of men. Because my father is the grandson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:46.260
of Leon Trotsky. And the only place that gave&nbsp;
political asylum to Leon Trotsky was Mexico.&nbsp;

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:56.340
And my mother who was born in Madrid, ended&nbsp;
up in Mexico because also Mexico was the place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:04.800
that gave asylum to the children and of the&nbsp;
people that were fighting against Franco in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:12.840
Madrid. So Madrid was going to be bombarded by the&nbsp;
Franco forces, so the government of Mexico, in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:20.280
attempt to save those children, offered asylum&nbsp;
to the children to be brought from Madrid into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:26.460
Mexico, at the care of the Mexican government.
And so that's how the two families came together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:34.920
My father came to Mexico as an orphan because&nbsp;
Trotsky basically had taken the responsibility of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:42.840
him. His mother had committed suicide. His father&nbsp;
had been sent to a concentration camp by Stalin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:48.960
And so the only relative that he had was Trotsky.&nbsp;
So he came to Mexico because that was his family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:57.720
And he was 12 years old when he came to Mexico.
And that's how he ended up in Mexico. My mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:05.760
came to Mexico when she was 18 years of age,&nbsp;
coming with her family to look for her two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:12.300
brothers who had come as little children as part&nbsp;
of the asylum to the children that were in Madrid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:21.360
And they met, and that's how fortuitous history&nbsp;
is. They met, fell in love, and then they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:27.660
four daughters and I was the second one of them.
And we actually lived in the house where Trotsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:34.500
had been killed because my father took the&nbsp;
responsibility to ensure that the house where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:43.800
Trotsky lived will be left as a museum so that&nbsp;
there would be a historical memory of what tyranny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:51.000
can do, the tyranny of Stalin and basically the&nbsp;
extermination of all of his enemies, which of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:59.220
course Trotsky was of his, one of his main ones.
And he basically aimed to destroy the family of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:07.020
Trotsky up to the third generation, which would&nbsp;
have ended with my father. But it would have ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:11.280
up with us, actually the third generation is us.&nbsp;
And they tried to kill my father and my father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:19.500
was a little child, but they didn't succeed.
So and then by my time I was born, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:26.340
the third generation, no one has tried to kill&nbsp;
me fortunately too, not from the Stalin era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:30.660
but I shouldn't be so flippant about it. I&nbsp;
mean, it's a very serious issue. And of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:38.220
it has followed our family. And at one point&nbsp;
when I was a medical student, I wanted to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:46.320
to Russia to study because, and I was given a&nbsp;
scholarship. And my father really freaked out.&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:51.840
And at that point is when I was basically made&nbsp;
aware and said, "Nora, you cannot do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:58.380
You're also going to be putting your father&nbsp;
in a very awkward position because he can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:06.180
basically blackmailed." And there is this curse&nbsp;
that Stalin had said that he would persecute up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:11.640
to the third generation. And that's how I ended&nbsp;
up in Mexico City and where I went to school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:18.540
and until I did my training in psychiatry.
Tim Ferriss: So I would love to expand on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:23.640
this just a little bit for people who may not&nbsp;
recognize the name Leon Trotsky. So number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:29.940
we won't go into a huge Wikipedia page on&nbsp;
Leon Trotsky, but people should read the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:36.960
basics and find him online. Very historically&nbsp;
notable, very important, as you mentioned, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:43.440
opposition of sorts with respect to some&nbsp;
very powerful figures in Soviet history.&nbsp;

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:47.460
And he also, based on my reading,&nbsp;
please correct me if I'm wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:54.660
but survived multiple assassination attempts and&nbsp;
then was finally killed, but not immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.660 --> 00:09:00.120
by someone with an ice axe that penetrated&nbsp;
his head by something like seven centimeters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:10.980
but still managed to subdue with his guards, the&nbsp;
attacker. And he just seems like such a force of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:17.340
nature and such a powerful figure to begin&nbsp;
with. And so to have that in your lineage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:25.800
I would just be so curious to know how all of that&nbsp;
has informed, if you feel like it has, the way you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:33.600
relate to the world, or even think about history&nbsp;
or voicing your own opinion, anything like that.&nbsp;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:39.780
Dr. Nora Volkow: Yeah, absolutely. And there's no&nbsp;
way it doesn't influence you. And be aware that as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:48.120
a little girl, I was living in that house. And so&nbsp;
I was surrounded by not just the knowledge that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:55.500
was my father's grandfather and I had basically,&nbsp;
well, until I was seven, eight years old, I also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.500 --> 00:10:02.400
his second wife was living in the house,&nbsp;
Natalia, who became a very dear person to me.&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:10.980
So when you are born into a house where&nbsp;
you know that you have a relative that did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:17.940
something that was very motivated towards&nbsp;
improving the best, the next generations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:25.440
the chances that next generations had, that they&nbsp;
were committed to a more just and fair society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:32.160
that is ingrained into your brain.
And both my parents were very systematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:38.160
making us aware that we do have a responsibility&nbsp;
that each one of us as an individual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:48.660
you should be recognized that we are part of a&nbsp;
social system where how you act has consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:56.400
and that your acts can actually significantly&nbsp;
improve or negatively affect the life of others.&nbsp;

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:04.860
So we, all of us, my three sisters and I, have&nbsp;
that very much ingrained into our motivation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:11.580
and how we live our lives. And so for me that&nbsp;
has been crucial. So I become very, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:17.400
particular. And this is something I've always had.&nbsp;
I very sensitive to people, the most vulnerable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:26.820
I mean, it just generates this drive and trying to&nbsp;
help, trying make I very intolerant of injustices.&nbsp;

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:32.940
I've always been. And so it drives me crazy.&nbsp;
It activates my amygdala when I see someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:39.720
improperly treated. And obviously as you grow up&nbsp;
and you have different, you find yourselves in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:46.560
positions where you say, "What can I do to help?"
And that has ultimately led me to take this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:51.120
position of, took me to take the position of&nbsp;
director of the National Institute on Drug Abuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:57.960
because I realized that it would put me in a&nbsp;
leadership position where I could influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:05.640
the knowledge that was derived in order to affect&nbsp;
policies that would lead to a better opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:13.200
for people suffering from substance use disorder.&nbsp;
So that's fundamental to me. And that came from my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:19.200
historical background and upbringing.
Tim Ferriss: What did your father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:28.440
feel the role or responsibility of scientists&nbsp;
was? I'm wondering, I suppose why he said yes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:34.500
to most things or all things science related.&nbsp;
Maybe he said yes to most things for you. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:40.920
have no idea. But if it happened to be&nbsp;
pretty specific to science, why did he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:45.600
do that? Why was it important in his mind?
Dr. Nora Volkow: I think a lot about it because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:51.720
my father, when we were growing up, he would&nbsp;
never, ever speak about Trotsky and we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:57.780
not allowed to ask questions because it was felt&nbsp;
that it was just too painful for him to speak up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:07.560
And the first time he did an interview about his&nbsp;
experience with Trotsky was when I was, I think I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:14.520
must have been 17 years of age or 16 years of age.
And that was a turning point for him where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:21.840
started to speak about it. And I think my father,&nbsp;
an extraordinary individual, I mean, with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:32.040
amazing resilience, was using certain mechanism to&nbsp;
try to deal with the suffering. I mean, his mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:39.720
committed suicide when he was living with her.
He lost his father to a concentration camp. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:46.200
sister was left behind in Russia because Stalin&nbsp;
did not allow his mother to take, yeah, basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:50.280
only allowed him to take one of the two&nbsp;
children. And so the mother selected the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:54.780
one that was younger, which was my father.&nbsp;
And then while when he loses the mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.780 --> 00:14:00.540
he goes and lives with an uncle and the&nbsp;
uncle is killed by Stalin while in Paris.&nbsp;

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:06.600
And then he ends up with Trotsky, and Trotsky's&nbsp;
assassinated. And basically my father is coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:14.400
into the house when Mercader, who's the killer is&nbsp;
inside and he hears him, "Don't let Seva — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:23.340
is the name of my father — see me." So he has the&nbsp;
clarity of mind to recognize he doesn't want my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:30.300
father to see him all covered in blood. So if&nbsp;
you think about what it must mean for a child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:37.080
to go these, all of these experiences, I think&nbsp;
you have to develop mechanism of protection.&nbsp;

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:44.460
And in that respect, my view is that science&nbsp;
provides it because science tends to be very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:49.020
black and white. I mean it's not really black&nbsp;
and white. I mean, the physical sciences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:56.040
mathematical sciences are black and white.&nbsp;
Biology is more graded, but you can extract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.040 --> 00:15:01.260
objective information that is reproducible.&nbsp;
And I think that gives you a sense of solidity.&nbsp;

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:06.720
And I think that this may be one of the reasons&nbsp;
why my father was so attracted to science. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:12.720
what I can tell you, he was very categorical. He&nbsp;
did not want any one of his daughters to go into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:20.340
politics. He was black and white, no politics.&nbsp;
And his perspective was that the family already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:26.760
had paid a very, very big price because of&nbsp;
politics. And so he really didn't want to.&nbsp;

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:34.140
So in that respect, of course, science offered&nbsp;
this ideal profession where people were driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:40.800
by looking and trying to understand the truth&nbsp;
of things in objective ways, where there was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:47.880
that subjective element where things can creep&nbsp;
in and rapidly be influenced by one person's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:55.800
perspective. Now, this is me speculating about&nbsp;
why my father was so driven into the science.&nbsp;

00:15:55.800 --> 00:16:00.600
It may just be that he loved science for&nbsp;
because it's a fantastic field. Science is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:07.740
basically you're moving your curiosity to try to&nbsp;
understand the world and they pay you for it. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:14.100
it's a great —. So I am making a speculative leap&nbsp;
because I have thought about it like you sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:19.500
asked me. But at the same time I have to recognize&nbsp;
that it may also just be that he loved science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:24.720
because he's a curious, very curious person.
Tim Ferriss: I'd love to look at other influences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:35.160
in your thinking and trajectory. And the name I&nbsp;
would like to invoke is Julian Villarreal. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:40.740
did check the pronunciation before we started&nbsp;
for people wondering. And I would love to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:50.923
who this person was and what influence&nbsp;
he had on you. If you could describe.&nbsp;

00:16:50.923 --> 00:16:59.160
Dr. Nora Volkow: Julian was a pharmacologist&nbsp;
that was working in a pharmaceutical company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:09.180
And he was a physician and a PhD in pharmacology&nbsp;
who had gone to Michigan University for his PhD.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:15.840
And I ended up with him because as a medical&nbsp;
student, I knew I wanted to do research. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:25.920
so I started to volunteer with actually one of the&nbsp;
major scientists in Mexico, Tamayo, who was doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:32.100
work on autopsies and tissue pathologists.
He was a pathologist. And he said, "Nora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:37.320
you like behavior, you like drugs, you like the&nbsp;
brain. Let me introduce you to someone that will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:42.960
basically have better background than mine."&nbsp;
And so he introduced me to Julian Villarreal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:48.540
And immediately, I hit it off with Julian.&nbsp;
And what intrigued me so much about Julian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:55.500
was that he was a — he thought outside the box&nbsp;
completely. And that resonated totally with me.&nbsp;

00:17:55.500 --> 00:18:01.860
And he was interested at that time, his&nbsp;
research was on trying to understand what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:08.100
were the mechanisms that account for the&nbsp;
tolerance to opioids and the mechanisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:14.940
that account for the withdrawal that emerges when&nbsp;
someone becomes physically dependent to opioids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:21.720
And his idea was that if you have these models and&nbsp;
you could understand them, then you would be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:27.540
to develop an opioid medication that would not&nbsp;
be addictive, that would not produce tolerance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:33.660
that could not produce withdrawal. And many&nbsp;
pharmacologists went into that whole field.&nbsp;

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:40.860
And certainly what I can tell you as of&nbsp;
now, no one has succeeded on getting an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:45.960
opioid medication that is not addictive and&nbsp;
that doesn't produce tolerance or withdrawal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:52.380
But the models that he was using and the way&nbsp;
that he was challenging me, which was very, very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.380 --> 00:19:03.120
quantitative, he was always trying to figure out&nbsp;
a way of quantifying behavior in animals that then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:08.460
could be used to predict the responses to drugs.
Tim Ferriss: How did Julian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:13.860
encourage you to think differently?
Dr. Nora Volkow: Well, he was introducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:23.880
me to a lot of the analytical methods to be able&nbsp;
to define variables. I realize this was very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:32.820
before its time. Right now, for example, a lot of&nbsp;
the analysis that we do employs vectors as a means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:39.840
to actually describe a trajectory of something&nbsp;
more than one point. And he was introducing me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:46.200
to those technologies and says, "Nora, learn about&nbsp;
it," and he would give me books, "So that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:52.320
try to see how you apply them to the measures&nbsp;
you are taking on the animal experiments."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.100 --> 00:20:02.460
So he opened, in my brain, the whole curiosity&nbsp;
of using quantitative analysis in ways beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:08.640
the traditional, very simplistic statistical&nbsp;
methods that we use. And this is something that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:15.420
now, is fundamental for the type of work that we&nbsp;
are doing, in many fields of science. But for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:23.160
as someone that is in neurosciences and&nbsp;
neuroimaging, it is crucial to basically enable us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:29.940
to understand and to actually integrate databases&nbsp;
that are very, very diverse and distinct.&nbsp;

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:36.540
So that, I owe to Julian. He would challenge&nbsp;
me. He would basically give me questions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:43.080
would make me think, and that's what I like.&nbsp;
And actually, he taught me that. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:51.480
something that I, as a mentor now, of young&nbsp;
scientists, I put them in positions where I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:58.200
force them to think and to think differently. And&nbsp;
for example, one of the questions, I always go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:03.720
you have students and postdocs, and they present&nbsp;
me the data, and I ask them, "Well, what do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:08.760
make of it?" And they give me an explanation,&nbsp;
and I said, "Okay, now, I want you to give me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:16.020
the most outrageous explanation that you can get&nbsp;
me of this data," in order to force them to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:22.680
differently. Because there is a predictive element&nbsp;
in a lot of the things that is a reflection of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:30.540
influence of others. And I want that innovative&nbsp;
way, that association that others have not done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:35.520
I want to encourage them to play with it.
Tim Ferriss: Right. Because as a mentor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:42.780
especially a mentor who is highly respected by&nbsp;
your mentees or postdocs or whoever it might be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:50.340
it would be very easy to correct them&nbsp;
and perhaps, in some respects, force your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:57.060
perspective on them. But that doesn't&nbsp;
help them develop the muscle of thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.060 --> 00:22:00.120
differently in the way that you're describing&nbsp;
by asking those types of probing questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:04.320
which makes all the sense in the world.
Dr. Nora Volkow: Yeah. But there's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:11.580
a selfish component into it. I think, for me,&nbsp;
it is totally uninteresting to hear a response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:17.460
that I already would have thought myself. And&nbsp;
I think that brains work better when you expose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:28.080
them to unexpected stimuli. So actually, I think&nbsp;
that scientific creativity is enriched by having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:36.300
that basically playing with ideas and concepts.&nbsp;
And I encourage it a lot. And so we have team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:42.540
meetings where I really probe them for people&nbsp;
to get into those spaces. And so it's crucial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:50.040
that you empower them, you make them feel actually&nbsp;
confident, because otherwise, people don't dare.&nbsp;

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:57.360
They feel intimidated that they may say something&nbsp;
that others will think it's stupid. And that's so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.360 --> 00:23:04.680
very common when you are in groups. People don't&nbsp;
want to be seen as saying something that will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:09.780
make them feel embarrassed. And to me, I always&nbsp;
actually start meetings and says, "Look, guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:17.280
we are here to basically push the limit. So please&nbsp;
do not feel embarrassed. If you don't speak up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:22.860
we'll never have an opportunity to understand&nbsp;
where that idea may go through." So it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:28.140
something I always push. Whether it is with&nbsp;
people are my mentors or I'm organizing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:34.320
with experts, I do like to get that going.
Tim Ferriss: So you have a well-established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:39.660
history, I would say, of pushing the limits and&nbsp;
knowing when to push. So I would like to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:45.600
read something I found from The Washington Post.&nbsp;
It's just a few paragraphs, and then I'll ask a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:51.960
question related to it. Here we go. "In 1984,&nbsp;
she," that is you, "left NYU for an assistant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:56.940
professorship at the University of Texas Health&nbsp;
Science Center at Houston, expecting to continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.940 --> 00:24:01.860
her research." And then, they're quoting you.&nbsp;
"'They had a fantastic imaging center,' Volkow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:06.660
recalls, but as it happens, "there wasn't a single&nbsp;
schizophrenic in the university hospital." What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:13.200
the hospital did have was cocaine addicts." So you&nbsp;
adapted. And I'll summarize a little bit here or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:16.800
just abbreviate, but you said, "'I was probably&nbsp;
the first person to use these new technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:21.120
for the investigation of drugs of abuse,'&nbsp;
she says, but her work was ignored initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:25.080
especially her seminal paper documenting&nbsp;
strokes in the brains of cocaine abusers."&nbsp;

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:29.580
And then, this is a quote of you, "'I&nbsp;
started to present these data at meetings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:33.300
and people didn't believe it, because there was&nbsp;
no evidence that cocaine was toxic,' she says.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:38.280
'That's fine,' I said, 'because this is what the&nbsp;
data show.' She applied for a grant from NIDA."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:43.140
Of course, everything comes full circle, but&nbsp;
"She applied for a grant from NIDA to pursue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:47.340
her research and was turned down; it took three&nbsp;
years before The British Journal of Psychiatry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:52.200
finally published her paper." And then, the&nbsp;
closing quote from you in this section is, "'This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:57.600
is what happens when you come up with things&nbsp;
before their time.'" And my question for you is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:07.080
what was the experience like? And what was your&nbsp;
inner talk like going through this sequence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:12.780
events and having people dismiss, having people&nbsp;
reject, over that period of time, what was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.780 --> 00:25:17.340
like for you? And how did you keep going?
Dr. Nora Volkow: Well, I'm a very perseverant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:22.920
person, and I never give up. So sort of&nbsp;
my perspective is on this issue has always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:29.580
been like that. And that finding, actually, I'm&nbsp;
still working on that to try to understand why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:35.040
how cocaine affects and producing these&nbsp;
strokes and how we can prevent them and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:41.880
how we can help people. But at that time,&nbsp;
I was very young, and you have this sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:47.880
of you trust the system and you believe,&nbsp;
"Well, maybe I'm not good enough." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:58.560
we haven't consolidated the belief in yourself,&nbsp;
because you just basically recently trained. No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:04.200
one had used that technology, and at that&nbsp;
point, there was this belief that cocaine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:11.760
was safe. So here comes this person and showing&nbsp;
these data on images that others had never seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:17.940
and with the background that cocaine&nbsp;
was, at that time, felt to be safe.&nbsp;

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:23.160
And I submitted it to The New England Journal&nbsp;
of Medicine, which is the most important medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:27.960
journal, clinical medical journal. They review&nbsp;
it, and they came back again saying, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:32.820
there's really no evidence that cocaine&nbsp;
can be damaging to the blood vessels."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:39.180
They were completely wrong. And now, it's&nbsp;
clear. All of that is history. No one will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:44.880
question that cocaine, as well as other stimulant&nbsp;
drugs, produces vascular damage and pathology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:53.280
But it is the way that science goes. And you&nbsp;
sort of say science should be guided by data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.280 --> 00:27:02.280
but science has a religious nature to it also. And&nbsp;
once a model is created, it becomes very difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:09.540
to change it. And it takes a long, long time for&nbsp;
concepts that have been consolidated, as accepted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.540 --> 00:27:17.340
as the truth, to be questioned. And I see it. It&nbsp;
happened to me there, but I see it constantly.&nbsp;

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:23.400
And that statement that I say, that I stated&nbsp;
there, is very, very valid. If you come up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.400 --> 00:27:29.040
with something that it's ahead of its time, you're&nbsp;
going to have trouble getting it accepted, and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:35.100
may not actually necessarily be recognized for it.&nbsp;
When it becomes everyday life, then, other people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:41.940
say, "Well, what's the big deal?" But yeah, that's&nbsp;
in the nature of the scientific progress. But as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:48.360
someone that is directing and allocating&nbsp;
resources for science, I am very sensitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:57.060
to ensure that, if there are situations where&nbsp;
there may be something that is very innovative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.060 --> 00:28:04.380
that others are not seeing, that we don't curtail&nbsp;
it. So I tend to, in many instances, say, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:10.500
let's give it the benefit of the doubt, because&nbsp;
if it is correct, it's going to be much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:17.040
transformative than if we just continue doing&nbsp;
science, that follows the rules of the game very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:21.960
very precisely, where, basically, what&nbsp;
you're expecting, you've already seen." So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:29.940
I do think that we need to push the limits. And&nbsp;
with open access to scientific databases, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:37.260
happening more and more, which is very exciting.
Tim Ferriss: Now, as you mentioned, you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:46.980
perseverance as a superpower. It is just a&nbsp;
statement of reality that you don't stop. And some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:52.740
people may not have that, constitutionally. When&nbsp;
you are mentoring or having conversations with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:58.680
say, younger scientists who are facing their&nbsp;
first pushback, they're getting rejected, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:03.000
having to try again and again, maybe they're&nbsp;
feeling demoralized. What do you say to them?&nbsp;

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:09.180
Dr. Nora Volkow: Well, basically, I say "This&nbsp;
happened to me." My first grant was basically just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:18.540
redlined, totally and absolutely. And I was very&nbsp;
depressed. And at that time actually, people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:27.360
the institute at NIDA said "Nora, try again." And&nbsp;
I was in that respect. And again, I do not take no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:32.880
for an answer. And this is a message that I say&nbsp;
to young people, "You should dare to do things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:39.840
And no one has the right to say, 'You cannot&nbsp;
bring a new perspective into things.'"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:47.340
And so I always say, "Be daring and persevere."&nbsp;
Just with all of my postdoc students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:54.840
they get rejected on a paper, they get rejected&nbsp;
on a grant. The answer is just "Stand up and jump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.840 --> 00:30:01.320
back on the horse." It's just as simple as that.
Tim Ferriss: And for those who aren't watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:07.680
any video, you are very tough. You're very&nbsp;
physically fit, you're very mentally fit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:14.460
and you walk the walk. So I want to just&nbsp;
give people further evidence that you very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.460 --> 00:30:22.380
much walk that walk. And I'm going to segue&nbsp;
now to perhaps a discussion of the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:28.980
between studying compounds and experiencing&nbsp;
compounds. And I was hoping you could tell the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:34.980
story of how you became acquainted with Demerol.
Dr. Nora Volkow: Yeah, that's interesting, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:40.920
I love Demerol. I probably shouldn't be saying&nbsp;
these things, but what happened to me, I was in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:46.200
horrible car accident. And I was the passenger,&nbsp;
and there was one of those hits that the car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:53.820
it was, I see the car does a 90 degree flip, and&nbsp;
the car in the opposite direction comes in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.820 --> 00:31:01.200
strikes me in the passenger seat. Seeing the car&nbsp;
coming to me, I did not want my brain to be hit by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.200 --> 00:31:07.440
a car. And so, I said, "Okay." I put my foot into&nbsp;
the car to protect my head and to get my head as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:13.680
far away as I could from the door, because I saw&nbsp;
it coming. And so I did not get hit in the head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:13.680 --> 00:31:19.620
fortunately, but the door came crashing. And I&nbsp;
was trapped. My leg was trapped. It was broken.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:24.960
I was under a lot of pain, and I wanted to lose&nbsp;
consciousness because the pain was so intense.&nbsp;

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:31.260
And so, finally, they had to come up with&nbsp;
the jaws of life to open up the door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:34.980
because they couldn't get me — they get me&nbsp;
into the ambulance, take me to the hospital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:42.660
and there, the doctor says, "Nora, we have to try&nbsp;
to move the fracture, so that we can accelerate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:46.560
the healing. So otherwise, we're going to have&nbsp;
to go for surgery. So we are going to give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:52.200
Demerol." And they injected me with Demerol. It&nbsp;
was extraordinary. It was not just the intensity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:58.740
of the suffering was disappearing — everything&nbsp;
was fascinating. And opioids make you sleepy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:03.000
And I was forcing myself not to sleep,&nbsp;
because everything was so extraordinary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.660 --> 00:32:10.560
And so it gave me an insight of how powerful that&nbsp;
drug was, that, despite everything that was there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:15.960
I wanted to be aware of every sensation.&nbsp;
And they admitted me to the hospital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:19.980
and I had to spend there three days or two —
It was two nights and three days. And I recall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:25.740
I was in the terrible pain, and they would give me&nbsp;
the Demerol. And it was this sense of well-being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:35.100
extraordinary sense of well-being from the opioid.&nbsp;
And that is the only time, really, that I've had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:40.560
a real ability to have a consciousness&nbsp;
of how an opioid makes you feel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:41.580 --> 00:32:45.540
And so, when they discharged me, they discharged&nbsp;
me on Monday, the accident was on Friday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:54.120
I was waiting on the car. And I felt&nbsp;
alchemical. Everything felt very unreal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:59.820
And the doctor had sent me home with two weeks&nbsp;
of medication for the pain, and I stopped cold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:59.820 --> 00:33:06.000
turkey. Lo and behold, I did have withdrawal over&nbsp;
the night, and it was so bad. I didn't understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:10.980
at first, what was happening. I was restless.&nbsp;
It was worse than pain. And sometimes you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:10.980 --> 00:33:15.180
to experience these things to understand it,&nbsp;
because you say, "What can be worse than pain?"&nbsp;

00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:20.700
It was just I could not be comfortable, no matter&nbsp;
what. So I took one of the pills that he had given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:26.220
me, and that was the last I took. And then,&nbsp;
the physical dependence that was responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:32.940
for that withdrawal disappeared. And that's it.&nbsp;
That's my story with opioids, which made me very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.940 --> 00:33:39.600
aware that I do not want to be exposed to opioids&nbsp;
unless I have horrible pain. Because I can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:39.600 --> 00:33:48.120
their allure. I can see how someone could easily&nbsp;
fall into that nirvana, that chemical nirvana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:53.280
that they create. And that's the story of&nbsp;
the Demerol. And that's why I say I did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:59.040
I have to be honest, it was an extraordinary&nbsp;
experience for me to have been able to feel what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:04.020
it was. But it was also a very scary one.
Tim Ferriss: Super scary. I had nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:10.200
nearly that severe, but I had a terrible&nbsp;
shoulder injury that ended up requiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:10.200 --> 00:34:14.160
reconstruction. But in the emergency&nbsp;
room, when I ended up in the hospital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.160 --> 00:34:19.620
they were overbooked, understaffed, they&nbsp;
had gunshot victims and so on, who took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.280 --> 00:34:26.040
precedence, of course. And so I was sitting there&nbsp;
with my arm dislocated, sort of sticking out of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.040 --> 00:34:34.380
chest, for hours, and they gave me morphine. And I&nbsp;
remember thinking two things. The first was, "This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.380 --> 00:34:39.600
is incredible." The number two was, "I should&nbsp;
never, ever touch anything like this again."&nbsp;

00:34:39.600 --> 00:34:44.280
And so, after my surgery, when they sent me home&nbsp;
and they gave me Vicodin and this, that, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:49.980
other thing, they gave me a whole collection&nbsp;
of different painkillers. And fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:49.980 --> 00:34:54.120
some of them, I took for just a few days, and&nbsp;
they made me nauseous. And I was very happy that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.120 --> 00:34:59.460
they made me nauseous, because it made me less&nbsp;
likely to use them. But I do have a, I suppose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:04.440
a firsthand acquired fear, and then, a developed&nbsp;
fear, just from the experience of people in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:10.800
family and my friends. What is the current state?&nbsp;
How should people think about the current state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:18.960
of addiction and overdose crises in the US?
Dr. Nora Volkow: Well, it's unfortunate. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:18.960 --> 00:35:25.380
wish it were different, but it is in really&nbsp;
a state of very severe overdose deaths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:30.960
that are very difficult to control and regulate.&nbsp;
And what we are observing is that we are seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:39.300
more and more people dying with very diverse&nbsp;
demographics from overdoses, which, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:39.300 --> 00:35:45.900
makes it so very hard to control. Because at&nbsp;
the beginning, the crisis started because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:45.900 --> 00:35:51.660
started to overprescribe opioid medications.&nbsp;
And people diverted them. And people also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.660 --> 00:35:55.740
who should have not been given them, because&nbsp;
they didn't have severe pain syndromes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.740 --> 00:36:03.600
became addicted. And that opened up the door for&nbsp;
heroin. And then we started to see a rise in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:11.220
more or less, of heroin injection, heroin and&nbsp;
people dying. And then, in 2016, there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:18.600
transformative, very malignant change, which&nbsp;
was the introduction of fentanyl. And fentanyl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:18.600 --> 00:36:24.360
we've all been hearing about it in the news.
It's a synthetic opioid that was developed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.360 --> 00:36:31.020
order to have a more potent analgesic, and is&nbsp;
widely utilized in medicine for breakthrough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:36.720
pain for cancer patients, for example. When the&nbsp;
medications that they have no longer controls the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:41.700
pain, you can use fentanyl, but it's also&nbsp;
widely used by anesthesiologists. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:41.700 --> 00:36:48.000
when you combine fentanyl with an anesthetic, you&nbsp;
actually can control and regulate much better the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:54.300
level of consciousness of your patients. So it's&nbsp;
a very useful drug, but it is, by its potency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:55.320 --> 00:37:04.020
it is highly, highly addictive. It's also highly&nbsp;
dangerous, because of its potency, it can make you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:10.620
stop breathing very, very rapidly. And it's a drug&nbsp;
that is very cheap to manufacture. You don't need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.620 --> 00:37:17.160
to cultivate any plant. You synthesize it. And&nbsp;
the synthesis is very simple. And because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:24.600
so potent, you need 50 times less volume of drugs.
So you can bring it through the borders much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.600 --> 00:37:31.260
easily than with heroin or any other drug that&nbsp;
we know of. And so it has both pharmacological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:37.620
effects that make it dangerous, but also, the&nbsp;
production makes it actually very enticing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:37.620 --> 00:37:42.240
the drug dealers. So they are incentivized&nbsp;
to distribute it. And that's what we've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:49.680
accelerating during the COVID pandemic. Basically,&nbsp;
fentanyl permeated all of the country everywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:56.640
And so they started to also mix it with other&nbsp;
drugs, like cocaine or methamphetamine. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:56.640 --> 00:38:03.180
more recently, with counterfeit pills that are&nbsp;
sold like Adderall, which is an amphetamine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:09.360
or like Ativan, which is a benzodiazepine&nbsp;
that you use for anxiety or sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:09.360 --> 00:38:17.040
or even disguised as Vicodin, which is an opioid&nbsp;
medication for pain. They sell them on the web,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:17.040 --> 00:38:22.020
and they are less expensive than the real&nbsp;
thing. So people, it's not just that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.020 --> 00:38:27.600
that are addicted to opioids can overdose.
Now, those that are taking cocaine can overdose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:34.200
Those that can take methamphetamine can overdose.&nbsp;
And students that may buy a pill in order to study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:34.200 --> 00:38:40.200
for an exam may overdose and die, because&nbsp;
the pill they got had fentanyl. Or a person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:40.200 --> 00:38:45.060
cannot get their physicians to prescribe them a&nbsp;
sleep medicine. They go and buy it on the web,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:50.220
and they overdose. So this is the nature&nbsp;
of the major challenges that we have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:56.940
And we are seeing more and more new substances&nbsp;
emerging, which is like, again, running in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.940 --> 00:39:03.480
treadmill, because we develop antidotes&nbsp;
for the overdoses from fentanyl. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:11.460
they are mixing fentanyl with an aesthetic that is&nbsp;
used in veterinary medicine, xylazine. And that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:11.460 --> 00:39:18.540
actually, xylazine does not respond to Narcan,&nbsp;
which is the antidote that we use for fentanyl. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:24.960
this shifting nature of the drugs that people are&nbsp;
exposed to, the fact that they are increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:32.820
more potent, and the fact that they are basically&nbsp;
disguised in many ways, is expanding the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:40.620
of people that are now overdosing. And in some&nbsp;
instances, we don't have good strategies that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:40.620 --> 00:39:48.420
can use to help them to revert those overdoses.
Tim Ferriss: It's such a complex evolving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:56.820
ecosystem of compounds, and as you mentioned,&nbsp;
there's so many incidental or accidental ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.820 --> 00:40:03.420
to ingest, for example, fentanyl.&nbsp;
And I know of many direct stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:03.420 --> 00:40:06.840
where people have gone to a bachelor&nbsp;
party, they try a little bit of some drug,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:11.520
that ends up being cut with fentanyl. I know of&nbsp;
two people specifically. One just dropped dead on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:17.100
the floor. One fell into a coma from what would've&nbsp;
been considered low doses of illicit drugs. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:17.100 --> 00:40:24.960
they happened to be cut with fentanyl, which is&nbsp;
horrifying. And in fact, my friend who died was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:24.960 --> 00:40:30.720
not himself an addict. He was collateral damage.
One of my other friends I grew up with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:30.720 --> 00:40:36.600
there are a lot of drug issues where I grew up,&nbsp;
was a heroin addict. He had developed just an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:42.180
incredible tolerance to fentanyl, and he gave it&nbsp;
to my other friend to help with his hangover. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.180 --> 00:40:48.300
my other friend had never used any opiates, and he&nbsp;
fell asleep and just never woke up. So the sheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:49.020 --> 00:40:55.560
ease with which the fentanyl, as you mentioned,&nbsp;
sort of permeates different corners and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:56.160 --> 00:41:01.740
neighborhoods of society, which does not&nbsp;
discriminate in terms of — or I shouldn't say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:01.740 --> 00:41:08.040
it doesn't discriminate, but it has infiltrated&nbsp;
every socioeconomic class. It's really pervasive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:15.600
and really terrifying. What is your perspective&nbsp;
on the War on Drugs and perhaps any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.440 --> 00:41:22.620
approaches that you think may be more effective?
Dr. Nora Volkow: Yeah. And I'll answer it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:22.620 --> 00:41:30.240
but I guess actually the story that you just told&nbsp;
of your friend that how he'd be taking fentanyl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:31.140 --> 00:41:37.020
unbeknowing what it was, it just killed him. And&nbsp;
this is what we are observing a lot. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:37.020 --> 00:41:43.920
it's important to educate people that this is&nbsp;
a seriously dangerous drugs. And unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:43.920 --> 00:41:49.380
I think that we have cried wolf so many,&nbsp;
many times that we've lost credibility.&nbsp;

00:41:49.380 --> 00:41:56.640
But if there was a way that we could convey&nbsp;
that in very objective, we don't need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:56.640 --> 00:42:04.260
exaggerate in any way. I mean fentanyl, two&nbsp;
milligrams will kill you. And the counterfeit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:11.160
pills contain five milligrams, some of them two&nbsp;
doses to kill you. So it is very, very tragic.&nbsp;

00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:20.100
But coming back to your question in&nbsp;
terms of the War on Drugs, I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:31.320
basically what it did was it created a mechanism&nbsp;
that could perpetuate structural racism. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:31.320 --> 00:42:40.560
very tragic to see how its enforcement led to&nbsp;
the incarceration of young black Americans.&nbsp;

00:42:41.220 --> 00:42:49.860
And again, that punitive system where someone&nbsp;
could be stopped, and this will happen if you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:49.860 --> 00:42:57.360
black and on the notion that you had drugs with&nbsp;
you and thrown in jail or prison because you had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:57.960 --> 00:43:05.760
drugs on you is such an unfair system. And it&nbsp;
didn't work in any way because it did not reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:11.940
the amount of drugs that people were taking or&nbsp;
the negative consequences of drugs to our society.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.600 --> 00:43:16.020
So it did not work.
And I think, and too, I mean one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:21.300
of the aspects that it did show is if you do&nbsp;
throw a person that's taking drugs in jail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:27.300
if you criminalize that person that's&nbsp;
using drugs, for example, when we did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:27.300 --> 00:43:32.700
horribly with crack cocaine. So if you&nbsp;
were smoking crack cocaine, it was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.420 --> 00:43:39.720
I don't recall exactly, but it was at least 10&nbsp;
times greater than if you were just snorting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:45.960
cocaine. Snorting cocaine or injecting cocaine&nbsp;
hydrochloride was much more favored by white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.960 --> 00:43:52.860
people. Whereas crack was favored by black people.
So cocaine hydrochloride, white, crack cocaine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:52.860 --> 00:43:59.580
black. Why do you see such a difference? It's&nbsp;
the same cocaine, basically. Cocaine is cocaine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:59.580 --> 00:44:07.980
and yet one could note a much higher penalty than&nbsp;
the other. That's an example of the consequences.&nbsp;

00:44:07.980 --> 00:44:15.060
But what science has shown is that when you do&nbsp;
throw people into jail or prisons that have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:21.480
problem with substance use disorders, then when&nbsp;
they leave, they are at much greater risk of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:21.480 --> 00:44:26.640
first of all, relapsing immediately into the&nbsp;
drug taking and escalating their drug taking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.640 --> 00:44:31.020
So you are exacerbating the nature of the&nbsp;
problem by putting them in jail and prison.&nbsp;

00:44:31.020 --> 00:44:38.940
So it doesn't work no matter what you do. And&nbsp;
that's why the whole notion of criminalizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:46.380
the person that takes drugs is basically very&nbsp;
negative vis-a-vis the outcomes of the person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.380 --> 00:44:53.460
and also in no way it benefits society,&nbsp;
it is actually very costly by itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:53.460 --> 00:45:00.780
When an issue becomes politicized, then it&nbsp;
becomes much harder to change and shift.&nbsp;

00:45:02.760 --> 00:45:06.900
And we've seen, I think, that we've&nbsp;
seen over the past, I would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:07.860 --> 00:45:15.180
certainly, five or eight years, an openness to&nbsp;
recognizing that the War on Drugs did not work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:15.180 --> 00:45:22.620
and that criminalizing drug users is basically&nbsp;
negative and it's promoting structural racism.&nbsp;

00:45:22.620 --> 00:45:28.920
Tim Ferriss: I'd like to cover quite a&nbsp;
few things and I would like to get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:33.600
harm reduction, but I'd like to do that a little&nbsp;
bit later and to hear your perspectives on that.&nbsp;

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:42.720
First, I wanted to go from the problems that&nbsp;
you've described to looking at pushing the limits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:50.160
daring to do things, some of the perhaps novel&nbsp;
or developing potential approaches to treating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.160 --> 00:45:56.880
addiction. And there are several things I'd&nbsp;
like to talk about. Certainly you have recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:56.880 --> 00:46:01.680
I want to say, written about TMS and deep brain&nbsp;
stimulation. I would like to talk about those.&nbsp;

00:46:01.680 --> 00:46:09.240
First, I thought we might speak about psychedelics&nbsp;
and psychedelic-assisted therapy and I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:14.820
congratulate and thank you for being one&nbsp;
of the first, if not the first institute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:20.280
director to fund psychedelic research with&nbsp;
humans after the scientific winter following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:20.280 --> 00:46:26.040
the Nixon administration, which interestingly&nbsp;
enough, at least I've read that some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:32.280
Controlled Substance Act and Nixon's decisions&nbsp;
around that were motivated by controlling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:32.280 --> 00:46:40.440
certain demographics or having the pretense&nbsp;
of being able to search and seize and so on.&nbsp;

00:46:41.220 --> 00:46:45.060
That was from, I think it was Ehrlichman&nbsp;
in his — it might not have been cabinet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.060 --> 00:46:49.200
but sort of inner circle. But coming&nbsp;
back to the treatment side of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:49.920 --> 00:46:58.140
could you speak to perhaps what we know&nbsp;
already about psychedelics as treatments?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:02.880
What is interesting and what milestones — I&nbsp;
think this is a question on a lot of minds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:08.640
and you're very qualified to speak to this. What&nbsp;
milestones still need to be reached to instill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:13.020
confidence in these drugs as therapeutics?
Dr. Nora Volkow: Yeah. And I think it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:19.320
very interesting, potentially promising area of&nbsp;
therapeutics, but the evidence is, the way that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:19.320 --> 00:47:27.720
see it is the evidence that exists is very limited&nbsp;
but is sufficiently intriguing actually that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:27.720 --> 00:47:37.140
cannot ignore it and behooves you in terms of&nbsp;
why we are identifying this as a priority area of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:37.140 --> 00:47:42.300
research in the whole issue of psychotherapeutics,&nbsp;
of the use of psychedelic drugs.&nbsp;

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:49.020
The data is strongest for the use of psilocybin&nbsp;
for depression, particularly for — I mean studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:54.120
have shown, and I think that even though the&nbsp;
sample sizes are not very large, they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.120 --> 00:48:00.720
consistently shown to improve the outcomes on&nbsp;
patients with terminal illnesses. Most of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:00.720 --> 00:48:08.400
I think if I recall correctly, were with cancer in&nbsp;
whom the psilocybin allowed them to accept better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:14.940
emotionally the fact that this was a terminal&nbsp;
illness and improved their depression scores.&nbsp;

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:24.540
And that was quite significant. There's also data&nbsp;
showing the potential for, and that was for PTSD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:24.540 --> 00:48:30.540
the use of psychedelic drugs. So this may be the&nbsp;
two disease conditions for which that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:30.540 --> 00:48:38.400
more data. The clinical trials are limited in&nbsp;
sample sizes and in numbers, but the effect sizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:40.140 --> 00:48:46.320
are significant despite the small sample sizes.
There are some studies that have been done in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:46.320 --> 00:48:53.700
substance use disorder. Nicotine is one of them.&nbsp;
Alcohol use disorders, alcoholism is another one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:53.700 --> 00:48:59.640
And again, both of them are on psilocybin. And for&nbsp;
both of them, there is also this positive effect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:06.960
And what's intriguing and speaking about it&nbsp;
in terms of the nicotine is that the effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:06.960 --> 00:49:13.620
actually appear to be larger than the ones&nbsp;
that you see with medications that we used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:13.620 --> 00:49:20.340
for the treatment of nicotine dependence, just&nbsp;
like Chantix or nicotine replacement therapies.&nbsp;

00:49:21.720 --> 00:49:29.280
So the number of people that stopped smoking is&nbsp;
actually significantly greater than that, that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:29.280 --> 00:49:35.820
the percentage that stops smoking with medications&nbsp;
that have been approved by the FDA. But a study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:35.820 --> 00:49:41.160
number one, only one study and the sample sizes&nbsp;
are small. So that's why we are funding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:41.160 --> 00:49:47.400
researchers. This is a group at Johns Hopkins&nbsp;
University to actually replicate that study.&nbsp;

00:49:47.400 --> 00:49:55.440
So the data is quite interesting, but it is very&nbsp;
preliminary, and I do want to emphasize this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:55.440 --> 00:50:03.960
because sort of people like to make conclusions&nbsp;
before the evidence is out there. And my concern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:03.960 --> 00:50:09.060
is that, and you already see it, you go into&nbsp;
the web and there's a lot of sites advertising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:10.200 --> 00:50:16.380
treatments with psychedelic drugs, not just&nbsp;
for mental illnesses but also for well-being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:23.220
when the data is not there. And so we don't know,&nbsp;
first of all, if first we need to replicate what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:23.220 --> 00:50:27.600
but assume that you replicate and it does&nbsp;
work, what are the doses, how to give it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:27.600 --> 00:50:32.880
what are potential negative side effects?
I mean, what clinical trials have reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:38.100
is in a small number of people, there&nbsp;
is a risk of suicidal behaviors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:43.560
suicidality. So who is at risk to show&nbsp;
that? Actually if you start to see it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:43.560 --> 00:50:53.520
how can you prevent it? Should you administer the&nbsp;
psychedelic drug? It has been believed that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:53.520 --> 00:50:59.340
psychedelic drug is adjunctive therapy, that it&nbsp;
is the interaction with a therapist while you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:59.340 --> 00:51:04.800
under the influence of the psychedelic drug, that&nbsp;
it is therapeutic. That's one of the hypothesis.&nbsp;

00:51:04.800 --> 00:51:11.100
So how you should be administering, how do&nbsp;
psychotherapists gets involved? And all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:11.100 --> 00:51:17.460
are scientific questions that we need to basically&nbsp;
explore with research because we don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:17.460 --> 00:51:22.800
the data there that is needed to build up.&nbsp;
And if we don't build up and people start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.800 --> 00:51:30.240
use it and they start to have negative effects&nbsp;
like suicides, then that can lead to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:31.080 --> 00:51:37.560
jeopardizing the future use of these medications.
The FDA may say, "This is too dangerous," or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:42.720
the DEA may say, "This is too dangerous. We&nbsp;
are not going to agree to its utilization."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:43.680 --> 00:51:48.600
And that is where the danger comes around&nbsp;
of pushing something to preliminary.&nbsp;

00:51:48.600 --> 00:51:54.900
Tim Ferriss: I agree that certainly the&nbsp;
positioning of psychedelics as panacea, which we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.900 --> 00:52:02.880
see a lot, is really putting the cart before the&nbsp;
horse in a lot of ways. And there are risks, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:02.880 --> 00:52:08.820
is why there are exclusionary criteria at places&nbsp;
like Johns Hopkins for certain conditions, whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:08.820 --> 00:52:16.200
it's schizophrenia or bipolar disorder or others.
And I'd love to chat a little bit about study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:24.780
design and what researchers might do or consider&nbsp;
to make their studies and grant applications more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:25.800 --> 00:52:35.340
appealing to institutes like NIDA. And the reason&nbsp;
that I'd love to explore that a bit is many smart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:35.340 --> 00:52:40.440
scientists are now paying attention to this,&nbsp;
including younger scientists who are considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:40.440 --> 00:52:48.960
this as a career focus. And as it stands right&nbsp;
now, there's a very small pool of serious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:49.560 --> 00:52:54.180
independent philanthropists or foundations&nbsp;
who are providing funding. It's very small.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.180 --> 00:53:00.360
I would say it may be in the 15- to&nbsp;
20-person range, really when we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:00.360 --> 00:53:04.620
looking at anyone donating above a hundred&nbsp;
thousand dollars a year. It's very small.&nbsp;

00:53:05.220 --> 00:53:11.760
And then on the other hand, you have, and I&nbsp;
think there's a role certainly, an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:11.760 --> 00:53:18.960
role for market-based, for-profit solutions and&nbsp;
providers, but you have sponsored research on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:18.960 --> 00:53:24.600
the other end of the spectrum. And NIDA has&nbsp;
been very forward-looking in the sense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:24.600 --> 00:53:33.180
you've provided funding to Dr. Matt Johnson and&nbsp;
so on at Hopkins looking at nicotine addiction.&nbsp;

00:53:33.180 --> 00:53:36.420
I would love to know, and I'll&nbsp;
just provide a little bit more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:37.080 --> 00:53:41.280
thought. I know this is going to be a very long&nbsp;
question and then to get your thoughts on what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:41.280 --> 00:53:47.640
people might do to make their applications or&nbsp;
studies more appealing. One of the challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:47.640 --> 00:53:56.640
is certainly that is brought up oftentimes is&nbsp;
blinding. And these studies or these compounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:56.640 --> 00:54:02.940
and the experiences associated are really highly&nbsp;
discriminable in the sense that most people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:02.940 --> 00:54:08.400
know they are either in the control group or not.
And you have potential active placebos and people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:08.400 --> 00:54:14.520
have experimented with niacin. Some people have&nbsp;
considered higher doses of, say, cannabinoids, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:14.520 --> 00:54:24.000
it's difficult it seems to find lookalike drugs&nbsp;
that do not also bring similar actions or effects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:25.620 --> 00:54:31.260
Do you think there's a place for, say,&nbsp;
comparative efficacy studies where you might have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:32.340 --> 00:54:37.380
by analogy, psychotherapy versus X, right?
The people who are getting psychotherapy are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:37.380 --> 00:54:41.040
to be aware they're getting psychotherapy, but you&nbsp;
could compare it with another therapy where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:46.260
could compare psilocybin for, say, nicotine&nbsp;
addiction to nicotine replacement therapy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:47.460 --> 00:54:52.020
Those are just a couple of, I&nbsp;
suppose, a bit of connective tissue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:57.600
for people who may not have more context.
But what would you like scientists to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.600 --> 00:55:03.000
attention to? What would you implore them to try&nbsp;
to do with their studies and designs if they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:03.000 --> 00:55:06.960
to try to get more federal funding?
Dr. Nora Volkow: Well, I think that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:06.960 --> 00:55:13.500
I mean certainly you've discussed, and there's&nbsp;
no simple solutions of how difficult it is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:13.500 --> 00:55:19.860
control for a placebo, but it is an important&nbsp;
one. So one of the recommendations is to use a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:19.860 --> 00:55:26.160
very low dose of the psychedelic drug. So you're&nbsp;
giving them the psychedelic drug, but a dose that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:26.160 --> 00:55:31.140
is considered to be subtherapeutic. And that's&nbsp;
what some of the researchers are doing right now.&nbsp;

00:55:31.140 --> 00:55:37.320
And to follow all of the guidelines and practices&nbsp;
when you're going to be doing a randomized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:37.320 --> 00:55:43.320
clinical trial, which are the ones that you&nbsp;
basically divide participants. Some get the active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:43.320 --> 00:55:50.580
intervention, others do not, and you randomize&nbsp;
it and you follow them. And that's the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:50.580 --> 00:55:55.800
you actually collect data that then allows you&nbsp;
to determine if there is a difference or not.&nbsp;

00:55:56.580 --> 00:56:05.520
But I think that the grantees that are coming up&nbsp;
with proposals know these, I mean, how to propose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:05.520 --> 00:56:11.460
one of the randomized clinical trials. One of the&nbsp;
things that we work with grantees too, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:11.460 --> 00:56:18.360
important, is that we help them in terms of the&nbsp;
interactions with the FDA because the FDA at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:18.360 --> 00:56:25.020
end of the day is the one that's going to be able&nbsp;
to say what data do they need in order to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:25.020 --> 00:56:31.680
to consider that a clinical trial is done in a&nbsp;
way that they make it feel comfortable to approve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:31.680 --> 00:56:37.860
an indication for a psychedelic drug or not.
So it is crucial that they work from beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:37.860 --> 00:56:43.740
as they are planning to do their clinical&nbsp;
trial, that they engage with the FDA and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:43.740 --> 00:56:50.820
absolutely help them. I mean, we try to provide&nbsp;
them support if the FDA, for example, wants them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:50.820 --> 00:56:57.540
to do toxicology X or toxicology Y or look at the&nbsp;
biodistribution. There are certain things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:57.540 --> 00:57:04.740
we can, through our contracts, help them obtain.
And we are specifically requesting this, and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:04.740 --> 00:57:11.700
speaking right now of clinical trials of grantees.&nbsp;
There may be other scientific questions that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:11.700 --> 00:57:16.380
very valid that are not just a randomized clinical&nbsp;
trial that we also would be very interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:17.940 --> 00:57:24.600
on funding. For example, with brain imaging,&nbsp;
you can now look at how, and there was a paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:24.600 --> 00:57:29.220
There's one paper that actually looks at the&nbsp;
effects of psilocybin. I think it was psilocybin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:29.220 --> 00:57:34.200
Either LSD or psilocybin, I don't recall.
And you're looking at, we have these brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:34.200 --> 00:57:40.200
networks that interact with one another, and&nbsp;
what they showed was that that architecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:40.200 --> 00:57:45.900
of network was significantly disrupted during&nbsp;
the hallucination state when the drug was on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:45.900 --> 00:57:52.200
board. And so if you can, I mean, do research&nbsp;
that can identify biomarkers of the effects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:52.200 --> 00:57:58.800
the psychedelic drug, that's one area that is&nbsp;
interesting. But also through those studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:58.800 --> 00:58:05.280
can you understand the mechanisms that are&nbsp;
responsible for the therapeutic benefits.&nbsp;

00:58:05.280 --> 00:58:10.500
So by looking at how the brain changes its&nbsp;
operations, and then you can determine if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:10.500 --> 00:58:16.980
that is associated with therapeutic benefit, you&nbsp;
can start to understand mechanisms of actions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:16.980 --> 00:58:23.640
psychedelic drugs. We're also funding researchers&nbsp;
to develop, we know what the psychedelic drugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:23.640 --> 00:58:29.520
that we currently have, but with chemistry, you&nbsp;
can create other psychedelic drugs, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:29.520 --> 00:58:36.480
that may have the same pharmacological target,&nbsp;
but they don't produce hallucinatory experiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:36.480 --> 00:58:41.280
that which will make it much&nbsp;
easier to actually implement.&nbsp;

00:58:41.280 --> 00:58:50.100
So we are interested in a whole gamut of research&nbsp;
that could help advance our understanding of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:50.100 --> 00:58:56.160
potential of psychedelic drugs for therapeutics.
Tim Ferriss: The entire category of sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:56.160 --> 00:59:04.680
non-hallucinatory psychedelic compounds is very&nbsp;
interesting. I think there appear to be some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:04.680 --> 00:59:13.140
very interesting indications that might be really&nbsp;
favorable targets for these, what are sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:13.140 --> 00:59:19.020
called the psychoplastogens, like cluster&nbsp;
headaches in the case of LSD or LSD analogs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:19.980 --> 00:59:26.640
What would you make, and the answer could be&nbsp;
that they're mistaken of researchers who report&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:31.800
based on all the documentation from subjects&nbsp;
in these various studies, whether at UCSF.&nbsp;

00:59:31.800 --> 00:59:39.300
And I think the imaging that you were mentioning&nbsp;
may be Robin Carhart-Harris and his team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:44.820
because they do a lot of functional MRI and&nbsp;
imaging looking at the perhaps increased entropy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:44.820 --> 00:59:51.600
or connectivity or both communication-wise in the&nbsp;
brain on these experiences. Anyway, in that case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:51.600 --> 01:00:01.260
it would be psilocybin. And what would you make&nbsp;
potentially of the importance of meaning making?&nbsp;

01:00:01.260 --> 01:00:10.440
And that is to say beyond the activity signature&nbsp;
that you can track or the receptor affinity the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:10.440 --> 01:00:17.400
reports from subjects that it's the meaning making&nbsp;
in these sessions, which seem to be, in part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:18.360 --> 01:00:24.480
hand in hand with the hallucinations, the&nbsp;
psychedelic experience relating to how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:24.480 --> 01:00:28.800
see themselves and the world that lead to the&nbsp;
behavioral change in the durability of some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:28.800 --> 01:00:37.320
these effects rather than dendritic spine growth&nbsp;
or any number of changes that you can observe.&nbsp;

01:00:38.640 --> 01:00:45.000
But given that, say, the duration of some of these&nbsp;
sessions is four to six hours, let's just say, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:45.720 --> 01:00:51.540
the half-life is pretty short, but some of the&nbsp;
effects seem to be quite durable with, say, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:52.620 --> 01:00:58.440
existential distress in terminal&nbsp;
cancer patients and also for nicotine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:59.040 --> 01:01:07.380
Is there a way to include those factors as an area&nbsp;
of inquiry in addition to the sort of mechanistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:07.380 --> 01:01:11.280
studies and the basic science and the imaging?
Dr. Nora Volkow: Of course, and there's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:11.280 --> 01:01:16.140
actually papers that have done that, that&nbsp;
actually have looked at the association&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:16.140 --> 01:01:22.080
between the intensity of the subjective&nbsp;
experience and their therapeutic benefits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:22.080 --> 01:01:27.000
and then have shown that those that have the&nbsp;
more intense mystical experience had, the better&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:27.840 --> 01:01:36.300
therapeutic response. But that element of meaning&nbsp;
and understanding is basically not in any way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:36.300 --> 01:01:43.680
exclusionary with that of neuroplasticity, because&nbsp;
it may be that in fact, that that experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:43.680 --> 01:01:49.500
that you have there while you are living it&nbsp;
under the effect of the hallucinogenic drug,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:49.500 --> 01:01:54.840
may generate a longer lasting memory&nbsp;
because of those neuroplastic effects.&nbsp;

01:01:54.840 --> 01:02:04.740
And again, highlighting why that combination&nbsp;
of the drug itself with the psychotherapeutic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:04.740 --> 01:02:11.100
intervention becomes crucial. So you cannot&nbsp;
certainly separate them. But to me, if we can, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:11.100 --> 01:02:16.440
it can be corroborating that there is this&nbsp;
neuroplasticity and will learn to use it properly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:16.440 --> 01:02:24.600
then imagine how extraordinary it is to be able to&nbsp;
generate a window in time whereby exposing you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:24.600 --> 01:02:30.300
stimuli under the effect of the psychedelic&nbsp;
drug, if it, in fact, turns to be the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:30.300 --> 01:02:36.120
you could strengthen a particular memory and&nbsp;
that would allow you to actually cover a memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:41.760
that may be very painful. So you are competing one&nbsp;
memory against the other. So I think that this is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:41.760 --> 01:02:47.760
to me, one of the most fascinating areas about&nbsp;
why I think that these psychedelic drugs deserve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:47.760 --> 01:02:55.260
attention for research because to have a&nbsp;
means to monitor, to accelerate learning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:55.260 --> 01:03:00.360
memory process, this neuroplasticity, long-term&nbsp;
potentiation phenomena that we've been studying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:00.360 --> 01:03:07.260
in neuroscience could be a very powerful tool.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's been very exciting to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:07.260 --> 01:03:12.360
involved with some of the early-stage science and&nbsp;
to see it develop over time and to also have a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:12.360 --> 01:03:20.580
of misgivings about the public discussion&nbsp;
when it veers into the land of panacea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:20.580 --> 01:03:25.860
because these things are absolutely not panacea&nbsp;
and they're very, I believe, powerful compounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:25.860 --> 01:03:29.760
based on the data that we have so far.
One more question, maybe two more questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:29.760 --> 01:03:35.160
about this, and then I would love to move to&nbsp;
TMS, which I am increasingly fascinated by.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:35.160 --> 01:03:41.940
I was a subject in a study involving TMS a&nbsp;
long time ago, but the technology was pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:41.940 --> 01:03:50.700
primitive then. My question is about substance&nbsp;
use disorders, and if in the very preliminary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:50.700 --> 01:03:58.800
but very preliminary hypothesis that psychedelics&nbsp;
and specifically psilocybin, so the same molecule&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:00.540 --> 01:04:07.860
appears to have effects across drugs of abuse&nbsp;
meaning or potential abuse, so if you have, let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:07.860 --> 01:04:13.980
say the work of Michael Bogenschutz and others at&nbsp;
NYU looking at alcohol use disorder, psilocybin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:13.980 --> 01:04:22.740
then you have Dr. Matt Johnson and nicotine.
You also have people like Peter Hendricks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:22.740 --> 01:04:27.120
looking at cocaine. If, let me not say if,&nbsp;
well no, I'll use if. If It turns out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:27.120 --> 01:04:33.720
those can be replicated, we can hopefully,&nbsp;
and I'm very optimistic that there'll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:33.720 --> 01:04:36.660
more federal funding for these things&nbsp;
because there is a bit of a chicken and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:36.660 --> 01:04:41.520
the egg problem in terms of sample size and&nbsp;
funding availability, which is challenging.&nbsp;

01:04:41.520 --> 01:04:48.540
But what could it mean if it turns out that&nbsp;
something like psilocybin is effective across&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:48.540 --> 01:04:54.300
multiple compounds? Is that common, I simply&nbsp;
don't know, in other sort of frontline treatments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:55.680 --> 01:05:01.080
or would that be something novel that&nbsp;
would raise very interesting questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:01.080 --> 01:05:05.940
in your mind about the nature of addiction?
Dr. Nora Volkow: I've been studying as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:05.940 --> 01:05:13.800
researcher multiple addictions. I mean cocaine,&nbsp;
alcohol, methamphetamine, nicotine, and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:13.800 --> 01:05:19.740
recently opioids and heroin also. I've been&nbsp;
intrigued about what do all of these addictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:19.740 --> 01:05:25.980
have in common. In fact, I've been intrigued about&nbsp;
what something like morbid obesity has in common&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:25.980 --> 01:05:30.300
with addiction if you look at the brain.
Because they all share as a common&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:30.300 --> 01:05:37.560
phenomenological presentation, the compulsiveness&nbsp;
of the behavior and the loss of self-regulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:37.560 --> 01:05:43.260
So you cannot stop it and you just basically&nbsp;
escalate on that behavior. So I've been intrigued&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:43.260 --> 01:05:49.200
about recognizing the common elements in the&nbsp;
brain and then of course also studying their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:49.200 --> 01:05:55.860
uniqueness. The neurocircuitry is basically very&nbsp;
similar. What is different is the way that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:55.860 --> 01:06:02.760
engage it. So to me it in and of itself, it's&nbsp;
not surprising that you have a therapeutic that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:08.520
is going to be valuable for multiple addictions.
And we're funding other types of research with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:08.520 --> 01:06:15.480
other molecules with the aim of basically&nbsp;
targeting just a medication for addiction in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:15.480 --> 01:06:21.600
general so that we could also start to address&nbsp;
the problem of poly-substance addictions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:21.600 --> 01:06:27.120
we have without having to just go specific.&nbsp;
So it doesn't, per se, surprise me. What I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:27.120 --> 01:06:33.360
would be very intrigued in that notion is if we&nbsp;
were to understand the mechanism and the common&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:33.360 --> 01:06:39.720
mechanism across all of the addictions is that you&nbsp;
create long-term potentiation in reward circuits.&nbsp;

01:06:39.720 --> 01:06:45.660
So you're strengthening the signaling on&nbsp;
systems that basically are associating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:45.660 --> 01:06:50.940
drug with pleasure and reward and you are&nbsp;
weakening the signaling that's associating&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:50.940 --> 01:06:57.780
pleasure and reinforcement for other stimulant and&nbsp;
reward. So just actually a strengthening process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:57.780 --> 01:07:07.380
and a weakening processing. And you generate it in&nbsp;
such a way that you've created an artificial need&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:07.380 --> 01:07:12.480
for the drug, just like you have a need for food,&nbsp;
now you have an artificial need for the drug.&nbsp;

01:07:12.480 --> 01:07:19.260
And I'm very much intrigued about the similarities&nbsp;
with pain. When you have pain where the insult is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:19.260 --> 01:07:27.900
no longer there, but the memory of that pain&nbsp;
continues making you feel it just like in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:27.900 --> 01:07:33.840
addicted person, the memory of the pleasure leads&nbsp;
you to continue wanting it. So it's not just that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:33.840 --> 01:07:39.120
I think it could be beneficial for different types&nbsp;
of drugs, but I do think there's value in actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:39.120 --> 01:07:45.780
even considering the extent to which, if there&nbsp;
are shown to be able to modify neuroplasticity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:45.780 --> 01:07:50.520
treatments for this chronic pain conditions&nbsp;
which are devastating and for which we really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:50.520 --> 01:07:54.780
don't have anything — I mean, not that I know&nbsp;
that there's much research into it, but I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:54.780 --> 01:07:58.740
just telling you where my brain is jumping into.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, absolutely. So a few things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:58.740 --> 01:08:06.060
come to mind. If you haven't looked at the work of&nbsp;
Gül Dölen, G-U-L, D-O-L-E-N, she's at Hopkins, her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:06.060 --> 01:08:12.960
perspective is quite different from, say, Robin&nbsp;
Carhart-Harris, and I think she has some very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:12.960 --> 01:08:22.680
interesting hypotheses around these reopening of&nbsp;
critical windows with respect to, say, plasticity.&nbsp;

01:08:23.520 --> 01:08:30.420
Then on the chronic pain topic, I will say&nbsp;
and this was done at an above board, very well&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:31.140 --> 01:08:35.580
run clinic with proper medical supervision,&nbsp;
but I went through two weeks of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:36.240 --> 01:08:39.840
ketamine infusion several years ago, not because&nbsp;
I was depressed, but because I wanted to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:39.840 --> 01:08:44.520
to speak firsthand to the experience because I&nbsp;
knew I would have a lot of questions about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:45.300 --> 01:08:48.720
from friends and also audience and&nbsp;
so on. I'm not a doctor, I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.720 --> 01:08:53.820
play one on the internet just to be very clear.
But what was fascinating to me is I went in having&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:53.820 --> 01:09:01.020
read a lot of the research related to depression,&nbsp;
and I've since had Dr. John Krystal from Yale on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:01.020 --> 01:09:06.300
the show to have a very long conversation about&nbsp;
all things ketamine. What I didn't realize though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:06.300 --> 01:09:12.000
when I went in because I was not going in&nbsp;
with any severe depression or I couldn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:12.000 --> 01:09:19.320
anticipated what happened, and that was for me, I&nbsp;
had chronic back pain in my mid thoracic back from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:19.320 --> 01:09:25.440
a longstanding injury which was very severe.
Had daily pain and intense pain for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:26.100 --> 01:09:33.600
10 years, let's call it. And when I left&nbsp;
my two weeks of infusions, I had no back&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:33.600 --> 01:09:39.480
pain for about six months. And it turns out&nbsp;
one of other primary indications is chronic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:39.480 --> 01:09:46.680
pain. There's a lot to recommend psilocybin&nbsp;
over ketamine at psychedelic dosing, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:47.580 --> 01:09:53.580
it matches very well with what you were saying&nbsp;
about the potential overlap of plasticity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:53.580 --> 01:09:59.340
not just for indications like depression,&nbsp;
but also for something like chronic pain.&nbsp;

01:10:00.120 --> 01:10:05.820
Dr. Nora Volkow: I wasn't thinking of ketamine.&nbsp;
I mean, ketamine is not considered, as you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:05.820 --> 01:10:11.520
a classical psychedelic drug, but there is a&nbsp;
lot of work on research ongoing for the value of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:11.520 --> 01:10:17.340
ketamine for the treatment of chronic pain. What&nbsp;
I would like to sort of look, and I look at it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:17.340 --> 01:10:23.460
in terms of what has been done, because again, all&nbsp;
of these areas, they're investigated but I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:23.460 --> 01:10:30.360
to check it out because want to see if anyone has&nbsp;
published anything on psilocybin for chronic pain.&nbsp;

01:10:30.360 --> 01:10:35.520
I was jumping into the classical psychedelics&nbsp;
which have very different mechanisms of actions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:35.520 --> 01:10:42.540
than ketamine, but absolutely ketamine. I&nbsp;
think ketamine was that drug that let us open&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:42.540 --> 01:10:51.180
up the doors to say, here we have a drug that is a&nbsp;
dissociative type of drug that is also a drug that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:51.780 --> 01:10:58.800
can produce addiction, a classical psychedelic&nbsp;
stone, and yet, despite everybody's prejudices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:58.800 --> 01:11:05.760
show to be beneficial for treatment of depression.&nbsp;
So I think that that is an example about why we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:05.760 --> 01:11:11.820
cannot just close our eyes and say no. We need to&nbsp;
look at these classical psychedelics. So I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:11.820 --> 01:11:17.760
like — you made me now curious about it.
Tim Ferriss: Also, the fact, as you were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:17.760 --> 01:11:23.100
mentioning earlier, that there is room&nbsp;
for potentially stripping out some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:23.100 --> 01:11:27.060
psychedelic effects. I think we need to keep&nbsp;
in mind that the business motivations may not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:27.060 --> 01:11:31.200
always be aligned with clinical outcomes. Of&nbsp;
course it would be much more convenient to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:31.200 --> 01:11:35.640
work with these compounds if they didn't elicit&nbsp;
the types of hallucinations we've discussed.&nbsp;

01:11:35.640 --> 01:11:41.580
But even something that is a non-classical&nbsp;
psychedelic, let's say Salvinorin A, so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:41.580 --> 01:11:48.840
have a kappa-opioid agonist, may have some really&nbsp;
interesting applications for minimizing damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:48.840 --> 01:11:56.580
after stroke, for vascular problems. Super, super&nbsp;
fascinating, of course more research is needed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:57.120 --> 01:12:05.940
So I wanted to shift to TMS and deep brain&nbsp;
stimulation and this is very interesting to me.&nbsp;

01:12:05.940 --> 01:12:14.670
We were talking about sort of different activity&nbsp;
or maybe pathological activity in different neuro&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:14.670 --> 01:12:20.520
anatomical structures. I don't know if the nucleus&nbsp;
accumbens fits into both that prior discussion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:20.520 --> 01:12:27.000
the TMS and deep brain stimulation, but could&nbsp;
you speak to what TMS is, what deep brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:27.000 --> 01:12:33.060
stimulation is, and why they are of interest?
Dr. Nora Volkow: Oh, they're incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:33.060 --> 01:12:37.140
interesting and I'm going to introduce you&nbsp;
to a new technology that has my brain even&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:37.140 --> 01:12:45.000
more excited than TMS. TMS goes for transcranial&nbsp;
magnetic stimulation and it's a technology that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:45.000 --> 01:12:50.400
is not invasive. You actually administer&nbsp;
magnetic currents in the scalp and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:50.400 --> 01:12:56.040
these magnetic currents actually go&nbsp;
into the cortex of the brain and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:56.040 --> 01:13:02.580
are signal transferred as electrical signals.
And it can be used to modulate either at high&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:02.580 --> 01:13:08.880
frequencies to stimulate or lower frequencies&nbsp;
to inhibit the areas that you are targeting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:09.780 --> 01:13:17.040
By understanding how in the cortex — the brain&nbsp;
is a very complicated network and so it links&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:17.040 --> 01:13:22.740
with almost everything else, but there are hubs,&nbsp;
there are areas that act as hubs. That is they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:22.740 --> 01:13:28.800
have many more connections. Some of them go deep&nbsp;
into the brain. So as you understand better that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:28.800 --> 01:13:34.140
pattern of connectivity, you can deliver&nbsp;
TMS in such a way that you can start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:34.140 --> 01:13:39.180
reach areas that are deeper in the brain,&nbsp;
which are very important for diseases of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:39.180 --> 01:13:44.280
depression and diseases of certainly addiction.
I mean you were speaking about the nucleus&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:44.280 --> 01:13:50.520
accumbens, which is the main reward region of the&nbsp;
brain and all of the drugs of abuse, all of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:50.520 --> 01:13:56.220
stimulate it. When you stimulate it in animals&nbsp;
with electrical currents or with optogenetics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:56.220 --> 01:14:02.160
you generate rewarding signals. The transcranial&nbsp;
magnetic stimulation cannot reach deep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:02.160 --> 01:14:07.440
but you can reach it indirectly. That's what&nbsp;
they use it and it's a technology that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:07.440 --> 01:14:13.320
approved for the treatment of depression.
Deep brain stimulation then basically is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:13.320 --> 01:14:20.400
invasive procedure. You put the electrodes inside&nbsp;
the brain and it's being used, for example, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:20.400 --> 01:14:25.680
treatment of addiction research. It's being used&nbsp;
for research, also treatment of depression and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:25.680 --> 01:14:32.520
other diseases. And the nucleus accumbens is one&nbsp;
of the main targets both for depression as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:32.520 --> 01:14:39.240
for addiction. But there are other neuromodulation&nbsp;
technologies, so transcranial magnetic, the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:39.240 --> 01:14:45.000
one is direct electrical current stimulation,&nbsp;
which instead of giving magnetic signals from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:45.000 --> 01:14:50.280
the outside, you give the electrical pulses.
But the one that I am very intrigued, and I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:50.280 --> 01:14:57.900
to actually introduce you to if you haven't heard&nbsp;
about it, is low-intensity ultrasound. So it uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:57.900 --> 01:15:03.780
ultrasound and you can use it with high-intensity&nbsp;
or low-intensity and you apply it on the outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:03.780 --> 01:15:11.100
of the brain. If you use high-intensity, you&nbsp;
can very specifically create an ablation. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:11.100 --> 01:15:16.860
you can do what you could do with surgery, but&nbsp;
very precisely without having to open the head.&nbsp;

01:15:16.860 --> 01:15:22.380
But that's high-intensity. The beauty of the&nbsp;
low-intensity is you do not produce damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:22.380 --> 01:15:29.040
to the tissue, but you can modify it for a&nbsp;
long time. So they are doing research right&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:29.040 --> 01:15:33.900
now and they basically approve — actually, the&nbsp;
high-intensity ultrasound was recently approved&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:33.900 --> 01:15:36.463
for Parkinson's disease and they are now doing —
Tim Ferriss: This is the high-intensity?&nbsp;

01:15:36.463 --> 01:15:40.440
Dr. Nora Volkow: The high-intensity, but the&nbsp;
researchers are looking at low-intensity for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:40.440 --> 01:15:47.040
neurological diseases and for addiction and&nbsp;
other mental illnesses. The beauty is that sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:47.820 --> 01:15:53.100
goes through the tissue so you can target&nbsp;
the nucleus accumbens directly with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:53.100 --> 01:15:56.100
much greater precision than with&nbsp;
transcranial magnetic stimulation.&nbsp;

01:15:56.100 --> 01:16:00.480
Tim Ferriss: That's super interesting.
Dr. Nora Volkow: It's fascinating. And you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:00.480 --> 01:16:07.860
need to apply it for 10 minutes. The pilot data is&nbsp;
showing that you see it for, say, the effects are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:07.860 --> 01:16:12.343
two weeks or four weeks with a single stimulation.
Tim Ferriss: With a single session of 10 minutes?&nbsp;

01:16:12.343 --> 01:16:13.620
Dr. Nora Volkow: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: Wow.&nbsp;

01:16:13.620 --> 01:16:16.003
Dr. Nora Volkow: The pilot data is very —
Tim Ferriss: That's very interesting.&nbsp;

01:16:16.003 --> 01:16:20.580
Dr. Nora Volkow: It's pilot data, sort of. That's&nbsp;
why I say, now that you're speaking to me and last&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:20.580 --> 01:16:27.000
time we spoke, I'm very excited of TMS. I'm not&nbsp;
in any way going to minimize it, but actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:27.000 --> 01:16:33.240
what I like so much, and I'm just going to say&nbsp;
what you push the limit and I said, "Let's push&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:33.240 --> 01:16:41.460
our own thinking." If you are able to do this&nbsp;
long-lasting effect as you learn to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:41.460 --> 01:16:47.880
better how these frequencies for ultrasound&nbsp;
stimulation affect the tissue, I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:47.880 --> 01:16:53.100
one day we may be able to cure addiction. No one&nbsp;
has ever heard me speak about curing addiction.&nbsp;

01:16:54.480 --> 01:16:59.580
Why do I think it is possible? Because we know&nbsp;
that when people have strokes in certain areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:59.580 --> 01:17:06.900
of the brain, particularly frequently involving&nbsp;
the insula, there are cases where people just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:06.900 --> 01:17:13.440
stop taking the drug. They forget about taking&nbsp;
the drug, they don't care anymore about it. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:13.440 --> 01:17:20.160
there's been instances which have shown that&nbsp;
the insult, a stroke in a particular area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:20.160 --> 01:17:25.320
cures the person of their addiction.
So what about if we gain that knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:25.320 --> 01:17:32.100
about what are the relevant circuits and areas,&nbsp;
and we have the tools now to manipulate them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:32.100 --> 01:17:39.120
ways that are much more effective than we had&nbsp;
in the past, that opens up the possibility in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:39.120 --> 01:17:45.900
the future that we may be able to cure addiction.
Tim Ferriss: Well, that makes me feel much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:45.900 --> 01:17:53.640
optimistic. I also feel like at some point, and&nbsp;
I'm getting ahead of myself, but looking at these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:55.560 --> 01:18:02.160
novel cutting edge technologies, or in some&nbsp;
cases very old technologies like psilocybin&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:02.160 --> 01:18:07.860
being applied to conditions in novel ways&nbsp;
that at some point there might be poly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:07.860 --> 01:18:13.920
therapies that combine some of these factors.
Let me ask you a question about the Parkinson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:13.920 --> 01:18:19.200
for a second though, because I have Parkinson's&nbsp;
on one, I think, but now both sides of my family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:19.200 --> 01:18:24.360
so it's of intense interest. If they're using high&nbsp;
frequency, and for those who don't know the term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:24.360 --> 01:18:27.780
ablation, and I want you to please correct&nbsp;
me if I'm wrong, but that can be surgical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:27.780 --> 01:18:32.400
removal of tissue or I suppose destruction&nbsp;
of tissue, might be another way to put it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:33.300 --> 01:18:37.020
I want you to correct my definition if&nbsp;
I'm getting it wrong, but how would that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:38.100 --> 01:18:45.720
strength of effect be used in Parkinson's?
Dr. Nora Volkow: It is the high-intensity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:45.720 --> 01:18:51.960
ultrasound and the high-intensity ultrasound&nbsp;
becomes ablative. So you are targeting, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:51.960 --> 01:18:58.380
example, the subthalamic nuclei just like you do&nbsp;
with surgery, but in this case you're not opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:58.380 --> 01:19:04.800
it up and you can very precisely damage it.
This brings psychosurgery to a completely new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:04.800 --> 01:19:11.520
level. And so they applied it. I think that&nbsp;
one of the targets is a subthalamic nuclei,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:11.520 --> 01:19:18.960
but I also think that they targeted parts of a&nbsp;
very small nuclei in the thalamus too. So it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:18.960 --> 01:19:25.740
fascinating also from that perspective in&nbsp;
instances where psychosurgery may be valuable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:25.740 --> 01:19:31.860
that you can do it as minimally invasive&nbsp;
and as precisely and in as small an area&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:31.860 --> 01:19:37.200
as possible. So I think it's quite remarkable.
Tim Ferriss: Now you've got me all excited about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:37.200 --> 01:19:44.220
this low-intensity ultrasound. I've been following&nbsp;
the TMS pretty closely, and in particular the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:44.220 --> 01:19:51.360
shorter duration, higher-frequency dosing of TMS.&nbsp;
So let's just say multiple sessions a day over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:51.360 --> 01:19:58.200
span of five days, there's some interesting work&nbsp;
being done. I know the plural of anecdote does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:58.200 --> 01:20:04.200
equal data, but anecdotally I've seen a number of&nbsp;
outcomes with friends' children who are patients&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:04.200 --> 01:20:08.460
that have practically just defied belief.
But on the low-intensity ultrasound side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:08.460 --> 01:20:13.620
you mentioned, and we'll put links in the show&nbsp;
notes for people and maybe I'll get some links&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:13.620 --> 01:20:17.280
from you or your team just where people can&nbsp;
learn more about this. I know it's pilot data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:17.280 --> 01:20:23.460
so kind of hold your horses, the jury's still out.&nbsp;
But nonetheless, very interesting place to begin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:25.860 --> 01:20:28.980
In addition to the durability that you&nbsp;
were mentioning, which I think was two to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:28.980 --> 01:20:33.180
four weeks, let's just say after a single&nbsp;
10-minute session, is the onset of effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:34.140 --> 01:20:39.300
immediate or near immediate as well?
Dr. Nora Volkow: It's basically almost immediate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:40.380 --> 01:20:45.900
One of the consistent findings that they're seeing&nbsp;
is that they see a reduction in anxiety, they see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:45.900 --> 01:20:53.400
a reduction in craving. It's pilot work. I want&nbsp;
to reiterate that, it needs to be replicated, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:54.360 --> 01:21:00.660
when I saw that pilot data, I said, "Oh, my God."&nbsp;
It was the group at West Virginia who has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:00.660 --> 01:21:08.220
doing basically the deep brain stimulation for the&nbsp;
treatment of opioid addiction. They insert these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:08.220 --> 01:21:15.060
electrodes and then they've basically observed&nbsp;
that in three of the four individuals that they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:15.060 --> 01:21:21.900
treated on that pilot study, basically they were&nbsp;
able to, again, very dramatically reduce anxiety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:21.900 --> 01:21:29.280
and they decreased the consumption of opioids.&nbsp;
So it showed a positive effect in three of four.&nbsp;

01:21:30.300 --> 01:21:35.880
I mean, they basically told me these effects that&nbsp;
we are getting with a low-intensity ultrasound&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:35.880 --> 01:21:40.500
that they've tested eight people are as&nbsp;
dramatic or more dramatic than when we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:40.500 --> 01:21:45.900
seeing with basically the deep brain stimulation&nbsp;
of the nucleus accumbens. So they target also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:45.900 --> 01:21:52.200
nucleus accumbens with low-intensity ultrasound.&nbsp;
So it is very intriguing. I'm a scientist and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:52.200 --> 01:21:57.480
love and I get excited with data, but I do want to&nbsp;
iterate, it's very pilot. It's very, very pilot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:57.480 --> 01:22:01.260
but it has to be studied. It's like with what&nbsp;
we were saying with the psychedelic drugs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:01.260 --> 01:22:06.000
it's very interesting, it has to be studied.
Tim Ferriss: Everything starts small. Almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:06.000 --> 01:22:10.380
everything starts small. We all start with&nbsp;
the pilots, so the jury is still out. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:10.380 --> 01:22:17.940
it's exciting that you're pushing the limits&nbsp;
and you're daring to push the envelope and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:17.940 --> 01:22:23.040
experiment with new things, of course. I mean,&nbsp;
that's part of the charter, but you also have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:23.040 --> 01:22:27.600
history of doing this well, and I don't think you&nbsp;
would've arrived where you are today had you not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:27.600 --> 01:22:35.760
been very dogged and determined. It just goes&nbsp;
all the way back to when you were turned down&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:35.760 --> 01:22:41.820
by NIDA and here you are as the director. It's&nbsp;
just remarkable. It's just a remarkable story.&nbsp;

01:22:41.820 --> 01:22:47.520
So Dr. Volkow, you've been very generous with&nbsp;
your time. I've enjoyed the conversation. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:47.520 --> 01:22:51.600
could keep going for another two hours, no&nbsp;
problem, but you have an incredibly busy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:51.600 --> 01:22:57.300
schedule. Is there anything else that you&nbsp;
would like to share with people out there?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:57.300 --> 01:23:02.743
Any requests to my audience? Anything at all that&nbsp;
you would like to say before we wind to a close?&nbsp;

01:23:02.743 --> 01:23:06.840
Dr. Nora Volkow: There are two things that I&nbsp;
would have liked to add. One of them is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:06.840 --> 01:23:13.920
addiction is very frequently comorbid with&nbsp;
other psychiatric disorders, and most notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:13.920 --> 01:23:21.780
is anxiety, depression, suicidality. This, I&nbsp;
bring it up, because you need to address these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:22.500 --> 01:23:27.540
comorbid conditions. Because otherwise, it's&nbsp;
not going to be possible for the person to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:27.540 --> 01:23:33.000
able to recover. And also, from the perspective&nbsp;
that, if you are depressed, if you're anxious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:33.000 --> 01:23:39.540
if you are suicidal, you are likely to take drugs&nbsp;
as a mean to automedicate, to escape that mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:39.540 --> 01:23:48.000
state. And it is crucial to send this message,&nbsp;
because actually, it's not as simple as saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:48.000 --> 01:23:53.340
that "You take drugs because you want to get&nbsp;
high and you're taking the drugs." People are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:53.340 --> 01:23:59.220
taking the drugs to, many times, initially, yes,&nbsp;
for curiosity or, many times, to feel good. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:59.220 --> 01:24:06.120
once you become addicted, it is really to escape&nbsp;
a mental state that is very difficult to stay at.&nbsp;

01:24:06.120 --> 01:24:13.740
It's very, in a way, very painful. And the other&nbsp;
point that I did want to bring too, that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:13.740 --> 01:24:19.680
haven't discussed, which is, in understanding how&nbsp;
we understand the effects of drugs and addiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:19.680 --> 01:24:26.340
and how we understand also mental illness, is&nbsp;
how important the environmental factors are and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:26.340 --> 01:24:33.480
how crucial they are, particularly when you're&nbsp;
a child or an adolescence. I think that we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:33.480 --> 01:24:39.060
all become much more sensitized to them with&nbsp;
the COVID pandemic, because we came to realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:39.060 --> 01:24:47.100
that there are certain people that were at much&nbsp;
greater disadvantage, and that's those people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:47.100 --> 01:24:54.300
are in circumstances of economic inequality,&nbsp;
of trauma during childhood, and what we call&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:54.300 --> 01:24:59.940
the social determinants of health, that are&nbsp;
really playing very important roles in people's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:00.600 --> 01:25:06.180
encounter with drugs and how they respond to&nbsp;
it and how they ultimately also maybe given an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:06.180 --> 01:25:10.260
opportunity or not to access treatment&nbsp;
and too how they respond to treatment.&nbsp;

01:25:10.260 --> 01:25:15.960
So as you were speaking, for example, with&nbsp;
psychedelic drugs, we are very concerned that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:15.960 --> 01:25:22.500
if, as psychedelic therapist, if they basically&nbsp;
get into mainstay clinical practice, if they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:22.500 --> 01:25:28.320
proven to be useful, that they are equitable and&nbsp;
they doesn't end up with, like many of the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:28.320 --> 01:25:34.320
fancy treatments that we have now, that are&nbsp;
only accessible to a very few. So that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:34.320 --> 01:25:38.640
only other thing that I would want to say.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, 100 percent agreed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:38.640 --> 01:25:44.580
both. And that's also part of the reason&nbsp;
that I hope we will see, whether it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:44.580 --> 01:25:48.960
with psychedelic therapies, psychedelic&nbsp;
assisted therapies, or other modalities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:50.460 --> 01:25:57.540
in some cases, options for group treatment and at&nbsp;
least the testing of the hypothesis that outcomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:57.540 --> 01:26:04.260
can not only be equal to individual treatment,&nbsp;
but potentially, even better. And I think there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:04.260 --> 01:26:11.880
is the possibility that that is the case, which&nbsp;
would also have the wonderful side effect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:11.880 --> 01:26:17.580
decreasing costs, quite significantly. And there's&nbsp;
so many questions of insurance reimbursement and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:18.480 --> 01:26:27.000
ultimately, reclassification, which would be sort&nbsp;
of a precursor to a lot of these things happening.&nbsp;

01:26:27.000 --> 01:26:38.640
But I agree with you, absolutely completely. And&nbsp;
it's also as upsetting and pessimistic as one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:38.640 --> 01:26:43.860
can become when they look at the compounding&nbsp;
of many of the addiction problems in the US.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:44.520 --> 01:26:52.080
I feel like we are at a point in history also,&nbsp;
with some of these burgeoning technologies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:53.280 --> 01:26:57.840
as you mentioned. And I think it was obvious in&nbsp;
the excitement in your voice. There are things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:57.840 --> 01:27:05.100
potentially on the horizon that could really&nbsp;
change how we, not only treat addiction, but how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:05.100 --> 01:27:10.920
we think about addiction as well, which is very&nbsp;
exciting and I think cause for a certain degree of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:11.700 --> 01:27:13.680
cautious optimism.
Dr. Nora Volkow: Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:13.680 --> 01:27:18.360
yes. I'm very optimistic, Tim, and I'm&nbsp;
going to abandon you at this point. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:18.360 --> 01:27:24.060
going to jump off that screen, but it's&nbsp;
been a pleasure speaking with you. 
 
 
 

